<<set $Random to random(1, 6)>>
<<if $Random == 1>>
    Between the foliage, one stands out – a warm, candid moment frozen in time. <span style="color:LightGreen">In the picture, a younger <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Hakeem</span> sits comfortably on a couch, his signature grin softer than usual. Resting against his chest is a much younger <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Isaiah</span>, his short dreads slightly messy as he leans into his father, smiling. But what truly completes the scene are two tiny bundles in <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Hakeem</span>’s arms – baby <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Jayden</span> and <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Kayden</span>, sleeping soundly, their identical faces peaceful and undisturbed.</span> <br>
    <br>
    There’s something genuinely tender about the image. <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Hakeem</span>’s arms are wrapped securely around his sons, and <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Isaiah</span> – though still just a kid himself – looks proud, as if he already knew the role he had to play it their lives. <span style="color:LightGreen">The warmth of the moment radiates from the frame, capturing a rare glimpse of quiet, unshakeable love. <br>
    <br>
    Even now, standing in the hallway, you can’t help but smile.</span> <br>
    <br>
    [[Continue|OC Miller House Hallway]]

<<elseif $Random == 2>>
    As you scan the framed memories lining the hallway, your eyes land on something different from the rest – a sheet of paper, slightly crinkled, its edges worn with time. Inside the frame, messy, uneven handwriting sprawls across the page. Hastily drawn music notes scattered between lyrics. It is set up like a plaque, with a title at the bottom in neater handwriting that makes you smile: <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“<span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Isaiah</span>’s First Song – Age: 6”</span>. <br>
    <br>
    <span style="color:LightGreen">Written when he was only six, the melody – if it could even be called that – was far from perfect. Some of the notes were scratched out and rewritten, and a few of the lyrics seemed to trial off mid-thought, as if the young mind had been racing faster than his hands could keep up. Yet, there is something undeniably special about it.</span> <br>
    <br>
    <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Hakeem</span> had framed it as a keepsake, preserving the raw beginnings of <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Isaiah</span>’s musical journey. <span style="color:LightGreen">It wasn’t about perfection – it was about passion! Even at six, <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Isaiah</span> had something to say, and this framed sheet proved that his love for music had been there from the very start.</span><br>
    <br>
    Standing there, you can’t help but wonder – had <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Isaiah</span> ever looked at this in passing and smiled? Or did he only see the flaws, embarrassed by the amateurness of his first attempt. <span style="color:Pink">Either way, his father had seen only the wonderful beginnings. And so, the song remained, forever framed, forever cherished.</span> <br>
    <br>
    [[Continue|OC Miller House Hallway]]

<<elseif $Random == 3>>
    As your eyes wander along the hallway, you notice a set of older photos featuring <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Hakeem</span> with <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Jayden</span>, <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Kayden</span>, and <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Isaiah</span>. At first, they seem like ordinary family pictures, snapshots of laughter and fatherly affection, <span style="color:Yellow">but something about them feels… off.</span> <br>
    <br>
    Then, you see it. <br>
    <br>
    <span style="color:Yellow">A woman’s face was violently scratched out. The jagged lines disrupt the once-whole image, leaving behind harsh, unsettling gaps where she used to be. The damage isn’t fresh, the marks have long since settled into the photos as if time had accepted their removal. A shiver runs down your spine at the sight, a deep unease creeping in. Whoever she was, someone wanted her erased.</span> <br>
    <br>
    But then, your gaze shifts to the other photos, the ones untouched by anger. <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Hakeem</span> sits with his sons, his arms wrapped around them, his smile unshaken. <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Isaiah</span>, <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Jayden</span>, and <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Kayden</span> are full of life, their joy evident despite the missing presence in these frames. <span style="color:LightGreen">In these untouched moments, there is no bitterness – only love.</span> <br>
    <br>
    <span style="color:LightCoral">And somehow, that contrast makes the scratched-out images feel even colder.</span> <br>
    <br>
    [[Continue|OC Miller House Hallway]]

<<elseif $Random == 4>>
    As you walk down the hallway, a colorful drawing catches your eye, framed like a prized piece of art. <span style="color:LightGreen">The bold, uneven strokes and exaggerated proportions reveal it as the creation of two imaginative boys.</span> At the top, in wobbly letters, is the title: <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Super Jay and Super Kay!”</span> <br>
    <br>
    The drawing features <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Jayden</span> and <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Kayden</span> as larger-than-life superheroes with capes flowing behind them. <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Jayden</span> wears a bright red suit while <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Kayden</span> dons a bold blue outfit, perfectly reflecting their favorite colors. Their arms are oversized and muscular for six-year-olds, and their stick-figure abs are draw confidently. <br>
    <br>
    The standout element is the villain. To the side looms a shadowy figure depicted in harsh strokes of black and gray. <span style="color:Yellow">Its face is unclear, with sharp lines replacing features, and claw-like hands reaching out menacingly.</span> Below it is written: <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“EVIL LADY”</span> <br>
    <br>
    Of course, the twins are winning. Super Jay and Super Kay blast the villain with laser beams from their eyes, accompanied by sound effects: <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“ZAP! KAPOW! TAKE THAT!!!”</span> They stand confidently over their defeated enemy. <br>
    <br>
    At the bottom, in block letters, is their victory statement: <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“NOBODY CAN BEAT US!!!”</span> <br>
    <br>
    The playful energy of the drawing is evident, but the villain feels oddly personal. Still, knowing the twins, they probably just invented a random bay guy. Whatever the case, it’s clear they feel triumphant over this <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Evil Lady”</span>. <br>
    <br>
    [[Continue|OC Miller House Hallway]]

<<elseif $Random == 5>>
    Among the framed artworks, one drawing stands out. Not for its precision, but for the sheer heart behind it. <span style="color:LightGreen">An old crayon portrait, slightly wrinkled from years of handling, sits proudly in its frame. Done by <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Jayden</span> and <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Kayden</span> when they were toddlers, the drawing is a simple yet undeniably sweet attempt at capturing their family.</span> <br>
    <br>
    The figures are classic toddler-style stick people, each one beaming with an oversized, lopsided smile. At the center of the drawing is <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Hakeem</span>, the biggest of the figures by far, towering over the rest with arms stretched wide – probably meant to show how strong he is. His hands are comically large, with too many fingers, as if the twins thought the more fingers the better. <br>
    <br>
    To his side is <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Isaiah</span>, instantly recognizable by the wild, spiky <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“cool hair”</span> they gave him, likely inspired by whatever cartoon or superhero they were obsessed with at the time. <span style="color:LightGreen">His stick-figure stands confidently as if their big brother is someone to look up to, even in crayon form.</span> <br>
    <br>
    And then, of course, there are the twins themselves. Instead of labeling their names, they scribbled <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Me”</span> and <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Me 2”</span> underneath their figures, unable to decide which was which. <span style="color:LightGreen">Their little stick arms stretched towards each other, a sign that – even back then – they saw themselves as an inseparable duo.</span> <br>
    <br>
    The drawing was far from perfect – some colors went outside the lines, and one of the heads was nearly as big as the body – <span style="color:LightGreen">but that only made it cuter and more genuine. It is a snapshot of childhood, a moment of pure love and creativity frozen in time. Even years later, it remains as one of the most heartwarming pieces in the house.</span> <br>
    <br>
    [[Continue|OC Miller House Hallway]]

<<elseif $Random == 6>>
    Hanging among the other framed memories, one photo stands out, bathed in the soft glow of the hallway’s recessed lighting. <span style="color:LightGreen">The image is warm, full of movement and life – the perfect snapshot of the Miller family’s energy.</span> <br>
    <br>
    At the center of the photo is <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Hakeem</span>, seated on the plush couch in the living room, his arms stretched wide as <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Jayden</span> and <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Kayden</span> climb all over him like two little monkeys. <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Jayden</span> is hanging off one of <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Hakeem</span>’s arms, his face lit up in uncontrollable laughter, while <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Kayden</span> wraps his arms around their father’s neck, mid-giggle. <br>
    <br>
    To the side, <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Isaiah</span> sits cross-legged on the floor, casually leaning against the couch. His grin is wide but amused, like he’s been watching the chaos unfold for a while. Despite not being in the middle of the rough housing, he no doubt belongs in the moment. <br>
    <br>
    <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Hakeem</span>’s smile is big and effortless, one of pure joy that makes it clear just how much he loves his boys. Small but full of energy, the twins radiate happiness, and <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Isaiah</span>, though more composed, still looks every bit like the proud older brother. <br>
    <br>
    <span style="color:LightGreen">The picture isn’t posed or perfectly framed, the motion blur as the edges suggesting it was taken mid-movement, probably in the middle of a play session that never truly stopped.</span> <br>
    <br>
    [[Continue|OC Miller House Hallway]]

<</if>>